In this section: some photos

* 2026-09_FinishedWheels: this is a set of pinion / wheels made in September 2026. 
    The pinions are silversteel, the wheels and connectors are brass.
    The parameters are: module 1.2, rolling balls 2.0, addendum height 1.0, dedendum 1.05.
    Note that, because of the fillets, we can reduce dedendum height to 1.05, instead of the more usual 1.2-ish.
    The pinion / wheel sets are going to run on 2mm pins, hence the flanged 5x2 bearings visible in the shafts.
    To manufacture, I soft-soldered an oversized wheel disc on an 8mm shaft, leaving enough length to hold the shaft in a collet.
    Then trimmed the wheel on the lathe, and moved the entire collet holder to the mill. On the mill, cut the ellipse decorations.
    Back to the lathe, machine the 8mm stub down to 7 and 6 respectively for the spacer and the piece to fit the pinion.
    Mildly press fit a pre-prepared disc for the pinion, using superglue.
    Trim the pinion and wheel to their thicknesses (using a parting tool for the backside of the wheel), but leave both pinion and wheel slightly oversized.
    Drill a hole of 4mm through the stub.
    Back to the mill, again by moving the entire collet holder. Probe for centre.
    Use contour milling to trim the outer perimeter of pinion and wheel.
    Mill the teeth using the MatLab code. See note below on silversteel.
    Bore the 4mm hole up to 5mm.
    Back to the lathe and part off.
    Where possible, clear up the edges of the ellipses, and use sandpaper to remove machining marks.
    Drum polish for a few days in 100 grade walnut powder.

Some key points: 
    Module 1.2 is about as small as one can mill with a mill of 1mm diameter.
    With the millpaths produced here, milling the silversteel was very comfortable. I used a VHM 1mm NaCo mill with short cutting length. On my machine RPM is limited to 3000rpm. Cutting feed about 70mm/min.
    In addition (for silversteel only) I used multiple cutting depths, stepdown about 0.4mm. (Multiple stepdown is not in the software, but can easily be managed on the machine.)

